<BaseLayout title="BL60 (Kunlun) — Kunlun Mountains Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat BL60 (Kunlun) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points/" }, { label: "BL60 (Kunlun)", href: "/points/bl60-kunlun-acupressure-point/" }]} datePublished="2025-03-05" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">BL60 (Kunlun) — Kunlun Mountains</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">BL60, known as Kunlun or Kunlun Mountains, sits in the groove behind the outer ankle. It is a primary stop for lower back pain, neck pain, headaches, ankle pain, and the kind of leg discomfort that travels along the sciatic nerve.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Bladder (BL)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Outer ankle, in the depression between the ankle bone and the Achilles tendon</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Lower back pain, neck pain, headaches, ankle pain, sciatic-type leg discomfort</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy to reach with your thumb while seated</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">30–60 seconds per side, up to 1–2 minutes</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 times daily</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/bl40-weizhong-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">BL40 (Weizhong)</a>, <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4 (Hegu)</a>, and GB34 (Yanglingquan)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Pregnant (traditional contraindicated point) or recent outer-ankle injury (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why BL60 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">BL60 sits in the groove beside the outer ankle and appears again and again across our body-region guides. The Bladder meridian runs the full length of the back and down the leg, which is why a point at the ankle can be relevant for lower back pain, neck pain, and even headaches — and why BL60 is a common stop in foot and leg routines for sciatica-type discomfort.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The point's name, Kunlun Mountains, references the great western mountain range of Chinese geography, a fitting name for a point classically considered to support the strength of the back and legs. Traditionally, BL60 is also a contraindicated point during pregnancy — it is used to promote labor and ease delivery, so anyone who is pregnant should avoid it. For everyone else, it is one of the most practical "the whole back chain is sore" points on the body.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/bl60-location.webp" alt="BL60 acupressure point location behind the outer ankle" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="800" height="468" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">BL60 is in the depression between the lateral malleolus (outer ankle bone) and the Achilles tendon, level with the top of the ankle bone, on the Bladder meridian. Sit with the leg crossed and slide a finger into the groove between the ankle bone and the Achilles tendon — the tender hollow level with the top of the ankle bone is the point.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) it is the soft depression centered between the outer ankle bone and the Achilles tendon, level with the top of the bone; (2) it is distinctly tender compared with the surrounding skin; (3) pressing it produces a deep, achy sensation that may feel like it spreads up the calf or down the foot — not a sharp pinch on the bone or tendon.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use BL60 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 dark:border-emerald-900/40 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• Your lower back aches after standing, sitting, or lifting</li>
          <li>• Neck and shoulder tension radiates toward the head or arm</li>
          <li>• You have sciatic-type discomfort running down the leg</li>
          <li>• The outer ankle or Achilles area is stiff or sore after activity</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You are pregnant — BL60 is a traditional contraindicated point</li>
          <li>• You recently sprained or injured the outer ankle</li>
          <li>• The skin over the point is broken, cut, or has a rash</li>
          <li>• You have severe varicose veins or a history of deep vein thrombosis in the leg</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: BL60 is a comfort practice for chronic back and leg tension, not first aid for an acute ankle injury — and never use it during pregnancy.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Lower back pain:</strong> BL60 relieves pain along the Bladder meridian, a common point for lumbar aches.</li>
      <li><strong>Neck pain:</strong> It supports neck and shoulder tension, especially when it refers toward the head.</li>
      <li><strong>Headaches:</strong> BL60 is a traditional point for occipital headaches that start at the back of the head and neck.</li>
      <li><strong>Sciatic-type discomfort:</strong> It follows the same line of travel as the sciatic nerve and is used for radiating leg pain.</li>
      <li><strong>Ankle and Achilles support:</strong> BL60 helps with stiffness and soreness around the outer ankle and Achilles tendon after activity.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage BL60</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Press firmly into the hollow behind the outer ankle, holding for 30–60 seconds per side and up to one to two minutes per leg. Because the point sits near the ankle bone, ease off rather than pressing into the ridge; increase pressure slowly to a deep, dull ache.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Sit with your leg crossed so your ankle is accessible.</li>
      <li>Place your thumb on the BL60 point behind your outer ankle.</li>
      <li>Apply firm, steady pressure with a circular or slow pushing motion.</li>
      <li>Hold for 30–60 seconds, breathing slowly, then repeat on the other leg.</li>
      <li>Finish with gentle ankle rotations to keep the joint moving.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing directly into the ankle bone or the Achilles tendon instead of the soft hollow between them. The target is a deep, dull, spreading ache in the depression — if you feel a sharp pinch on bone or a zing down the foot, reposition and ease off.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine BL60 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">The Bladder meridian runs the whole back, so BL60 works best with a routine. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 30–60 seconds each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Lower back pain</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL60 → <a href="/points/bl40-weizhong-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">BL40</a> → <a href="/points/bl23-shenshu-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">BL23 (Shenshu)</a>, 60s each, 1–2 times daily</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Sciatic-type leg discomfort</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL40 → BL60 → <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">GB20</a>, holding each 60s, with gentle hip and leg movement</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Occipital headache from neck tension</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">GB20 → BL60 → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4</a>, 60s each, in a quiet space</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Ankle stiffness after activity</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">BL60 → <a href="/points/sp6-sanyinjiao-acupressure-point/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">SP6</a>, 60s each, followed by slow ankle circles both ways</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see the <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-back-pain/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for back pain</a> and <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-neck-pain/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for neck pain</a> pages, and the <a href="/body/foot-acupressure-points/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">foot acupressure points</a> body guide.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">BL60 is usually markedly tender. Many people feel a release in the back and legs within a few minutes of firm pressure, though the effect can fade as the day goes on. Do not expect a chronic back to clear after one press — the first session is about learning the spot.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 1–2 sessions per day, tenderness at the point usually eases and back and leg tension often feels less intense. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for musculoskeletal pain, where benefits tend to appear after several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For chronic back or sciatic-type pain, BL60 works best alongside movement, posture work, and, ideally, physiotherapy. Acupressure is a complement, not a replacement. Pain that is not improving after 2–3 weeks — or radiating pain with numbness or weakness — is a signal to see a clinician.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Evidence on acupressure for back, neck, and musculoskeletal pain has grown in recent years. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for pain conditions, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Meta-analyses of acupressure for pain tend to show a positive direction of effect, though many of the included studies are small and their methodological quality varies considerably.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Researchers consistently note that acupressure trials are hard to blind, because the treatment is physical and participants can usually tell whether they are being treated. This makes it difficult to separate the specific effects of a particular point such as BL60 from the general benefits of touch, pressure, and relaxation. The overall picture — which we discuss in detail on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">does acupressure work?</a> review — is one of encouraging signals alongside a clear need for larger, higher-quality studies before firm conclusions can be drawn. For back and neck pain specifically, our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-back-pain/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for back pain</a> and <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-neck-pain/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for neck pain</a> pages summarize the strongest available evidence.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["chen-wang-2014", "hsieh-2010"]} note="These reviews evaluated acupressure for pain and for headache, the two main uses of BL60. Neither is a study of BL60 in isolation." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Avoid during pregnancy:</strong> BL60 is a traditional contraindicated point that may stimulate uterine contractions — do not use it while pregnant.</li>
      <li>Avoid if you recently sprained or injured the outer ankle, or if the joint is swollen and painful.</li>
      <li>Do not press over broken skin, cuts, rashes, or blisters on the ankle.</li>
      <li>Avoid deep pressure over severe varicose veins or a history of deep vein thrombosis in the leg.</li>
      <li>Stop if you feel a sharp or shooting pain, or numbness spreading into the foot.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Back pain that appeared suddenly after a fall, twist, or heavy lift</li>
        <li>Leg pain accompanied by numbness, weakness, or loss of bladder or bowel control</li>
        <li>Pain that radiates down the leg and is severe or getting worse</li>
        <li>Swelling, warmth, or pain in the calf that could suggest a blood clot</li>
        <li>No improvement after 2–3 weeks of regular acupressure and gentle movement</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Where exactly is BL60?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">BL60 lies in the depression between the outer ankle bone and the Achilles tendon, level with the top of the ankle bone. Sit with the leg crossed, slide a finger into that groove, and press the tender hollow — easing off near the bone rather than pressing into it.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press BL60?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Hold BL60 for 30–60 seconds per side, working up to one to two minutes per leg, pressing gradually to a deep, dull ache. Repeat 1–2 times daily for chronic back or leg tension, and stop if you feel sharp pain or tingling.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Why is BL60 used for sciatica?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">The Bladder meridian runs the length of the back and down the leg, and BL60 follows the same line of travel as the sciatic nerve. It is a traditional point for lower back pain that radiates down the leg. Never press into sharp, radiating pain, and see a clinician before self-treating persistent sciatica.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is it safe to press BL60 during pregnancy?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">No. BL60 is a traditional contraindicated point that may stimulate uterine contractions, so it should be avoided entirely during pregnancy — even though BL60 is also traditionally used to promote labor. Pregnant people should consult their healthcare provider about safe alternatives for back pain.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I press BL60 at work?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. While seated, cross one leg over the opposite knee and press the point with your thumb — no tools needed. A short 60-second press per side makes a discreet mid-day reset for back and neck tension accumulated at a desk, ideally with a short walk afterwards.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead of using BL60?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor for back pain after a sudden injury, leg pain with numbness or weakness, loss of bladder or bowel control, or pain that is not improving after 2–3 weeks. Calf swelling or warmth that could be a blood clot needs urgent attention — do not massage over it.</div>
    </details>
    </section>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Related guides: <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-labor/" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for labor support</a> (at term only).</p>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/points/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-emerald-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-emerald-400">Back to All Points</a>
    </div>
